{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Arthur awoke to the chirping of birds, a sound he usually found pleasant. Today, however, it felt… calculated. The sunlight seemed too bright, the colors of the garden impossibly vibrant. He sat up in bed, a strange stiffness in his joints, and noticed the pixelation around the edges of his vision, like a poorly rendered texture. A notification popped up in his peripheral view: \"Quest Available: Gather 5 Wild Berries.\" He blinked, convinced he was dreaming, but the notification remained. His bedroom was…generic. Beige walls, a standard dresser, a slightly lumpy bed. Everything felt pre-designed, predictable. He reached out to touch a framed picture on his nightstand – a landscape of rolling hills and a quaint village. As his fingers brushed the glass, a tooltip appeared: \"Item: Framed Landscape. Description: Decorative item. Value: 5 gold.\" Panic began to set in. This wasn't a dream. This was… something else entirely.\n\n***\n\nHe stumbled out of bed, the floor feeling strangely… responsive under his feet. He walked to the window and looked out at the town below, a collection of blocky buildings and uniformly dressed citizens. A character with an exclamation point above his head walked past, muttering something about needing to complete a delivery. Arthur felt a wave of nausea. He was trapped. Trapped in a video game. He cautiously approached the character, trying to speak, but the words caught in his throat. Then, another notification: \"Dialogue Option Available: Ask about quest locations.\" It was all so absurd, so unbelievably real yet fundamentally artificial. He had to figure out how this was happening, how to escape, or at least understand the rules of this bizarre new reality. The quest for wild berries felt insignificant now, overshadowed by the monumental task of understanding his predicament.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Arthur", "logp